❓Question
Given an array of positive integers nums and a positive integer target, return the minimal length of a subarray whose sum is greater than or equal to target.
If there is no such subarray, return 0.

Example
Input:
target = 7
nums = [2,3,1,2,4,3]
Output: 2
Explanation:
The subarray [4,3] has:
4 + 3 = 7
Its length is 2, which is the minimum possible.

✅Approach — Sliding Window
Use two pointers:
low  → start of window
high → end of window
Keep adding nums[high] to sum.
Once sum >= target, we have a valid window.
Calculate its length:
high - low + 1
Try to make the window smaller by removing nums[low].
Continue until the sum becomes smaller than target.
Then move high forward again.
Because all numbers are positive, removing an element always decreases the sum, which makes sliding window work.

Code
class Solution {
    public int minSubArrayLen(int target, int[] nums) {
        int res = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int low = 0;
        int high = 0;
        int sum = 0;
        while (high < nums.length) {
            sum += nums[high];
            while (sum >= target) {
                int count = high - low + 1;
                res = Math.min(count, res);
                sum = sum - nums[low];
                low++;
            }
            high++;
        }
        return res == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? 0 : res;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
